 People in a voice room can play card games together. The first and so far only game is Renk: a color and number matching card game for 2 to 8 players with a 108 card deck. Games are played only in voice rooms, and there are no games in private calls. Nothing changes on the server: game messages travel on the signal path of the voice room (`POST /api/voice/signal`), and the server cannot tell them apart from other voice signals. The person who sets up the table is the dealer, plays as well and chooses the rule set: Official, or Stacking, where a +2 can go on a +2 and a +4 on a +4. The choice can be changed until Start. If the dealer leaves the voice room, the game ends. If a player leaves, their cards go back to the draw pile, the pile is shuffled again and the game continues with the rest, and it ends if fewer than two remain.
 
-A game has four layers. `voice.js` only carries messages: a signal of type `game` carries the fields `{ type, sid, n, p }`, and `p` is a payload of at most 11000 printable ASCII characters whose meaning is hidden from the voice engine. Like screen and camera signals, an invalid game signal is dropped without using up a sequence number, and only a game message from a connection whose first negotiation has finished and from the same connection (`sid`) reaches the game layer. The voice engine reports four events to the game layer: an incoming message (`message`), a connection is ready (`peer-ready`, once per connection), a person left the roster (`peer-leave`) and the voice session ended (`reset`). `33-oyun-protokol.js` (`window.TelsizGame`) holds the message formats, the inner envelope, the counter rules and the randomness helpers, `34-oyun-masa.js` (`window.TelsizGameDesk`) the dealer and player state machines, and `35-renk-kural.js` (`window.TelsizRenk`) the Renk rule engine. These three modules are pure: they use no DOM, clock or network, and time and sending are passed in from outside. This lets the tests run them with real encryption in a four device network simulation with loss, duplicates and page reloads. `36-oyun.js` sets up the environment of this device and draws the general interface, and `37-renk.js` draws the Renk table. A new game is added through the same app contract: the id and limits (`id`, `RULES`, `DEFAULT_RULES`, `MIN_PLAYERS`, `MAX_PLAYERS`), the state functions (`newGame`, `validateMove`, `applyMove`, `removePlayer`, `endGame`, `isOver`, `turnOf`), the views (`publicView`, `privateView`, `legalMoves`) and their validators (`validateView`, `validatePrivate`). The rule engine is added to the `GAME_ENGINES` table in `36-oyun.js`, and the game's interface registers itself with `gameUiRegister`. The transport and the table manager do not change.
+A game has four layers. `voice.js` only carries messages: a signal of type `game` carries the fields `{ type, sid, n, p }`, and `p` is a payload of at most 11000 printable ASCII characters whose meaning is hidden from the voice engine. Like screen and camera signals, an invalid game signal is dropped without using up a sequence number, and only a game message from a connection whose first negotiation has finished and from the same connection (`sid`) reaches the game layer. The voice engine reports four events to the game layer: an incoming message (`message`), a connection is ready (`peer-ready`, once per connection), a person left the roster (`peer-leave`) and the voice session ended (`reset`). `33-oyun-protokol.js` (`window.TelsizGame`) holds the message formats, the inner envelope, the counter rules and the randomness helpers, `34-oyun-masa.js` (`window.TelsizGameDesk`) the dealer and player state machines, and `35-renk-kural.js` (`window.TelsizRenk`) the Renk rule engine. These three modules are pure: they use no DOM, clock or network, and time and sending are passed in from outside. This lets the tests run them with real encryption in a four device network simulation with loss, duplicates and page reloads. `36-oyun.js` sets up the environment of this device and draws the general interface, and `37-renk.js` draws the Renk table. A new game is added through the same app contract: the id and limits (`id`, `RULES`, `DEFAULT_RULES`, `MIN_PLAYERS`, `MAX_PLAYERS`), the state functions (`newGame`, `validateMove`, `applyMove`, `removePlayer`, `endGame`, `isOver`, `turnOf`), the views (`publicView`, `privateView`, `legalMoves`) and their validators (`validateView`, `validatePrivate`). The optional `DELAYED_MOVES` list says which of the dealer's own out of turn moves are handled with a 300 ms delay (Last Card and Catch in Renk), and the moves of a game without this list have no delay. The rule engine is added to the `GAME_ENGINES` table in `36-oyun.js`, and the game's interface registers itself with `gameUiRegister`. When more than one game is registered, the Game button first opens a local Choose a Game screen. The general texts do not depend on the game and call the person who sets up the table the host, and a game can give some of them in its own words (dealer, hand and deck in Renk) with `game.<game>.<key>` keys. The transport and the table manager do not change.
 
 The layout is a star: the dealer is the single authority, and players talk only to the dealer. The voice engine has no shared message order between peers, order is kept only per peer, so a single device decides the order of the game. The full state of the deck exists only on the dealer's device. The dealer checks every move with the rule engine and sends a full state to each seat separately: the public view (the seats and their card counts, the top card, the active color, the direction, the draw pile and discard counts, the latest events), the recipient's own hand and the result of the recipient's last move travel in one message, and no diffs are sent. A lost message is therefore repaired by the next one. The player's device checks the incoming state strictly (the field sets, the seat order, its own hand size, a card total of 108) and drops an invalid one silently. The rules are checked on the dealer's device. That is why the Wild +4 restriction is enforced automatically and there is no challenge step. The Last Card rule applies in both rule sets. When a round ends the remaining hands are not revealed, and the results show only card counts and points. Each round is scored on its own, and no total is kept across rounds. After a finished round the dealer returns to the lobby with New Round, and the seated players keep their seats.
 
